1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,547
The admin menu on the left is built from entries that modules provide.

2
00:00:05,547 --> 00:00:07,821
In this video, you add your own:

3
00:00:07,821 --> 00:00:12,428
a top-level item with two children, each opening a page of your module.

4
00:00:12,428 --> 00:00:15,420
Then you enable the module and use the menu.

5
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:24,461
The goal is a top-level item, My Root View, with two items under

6
00:00:24,461 --> 00:00:26,618
it, Child One and Child Two.

7
00:00:26,618 --> 00:00:31,440
Each child opens its own page, made of a controller action and a view.

8
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:37,797
The module references the OrchardCore.Admin package, and the site references the module.

9
00:00:37,797 --> 00:00:40,660
A separate video covers creating a module.

10
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:46,334
Start with the pages.

11
00:00:46,334 --> 00:00:51,086
The controller carries the Admin attribute, which gives its pages the admin theme

12
00:00:51,086 --> 00:00:53,980
and requires the Access admin panel permission.

13
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:58,292
The ChildOne and ChildTwo actions each return their view.

14
00:00:58,292 --> 00:01:02,646
Orchard Core maps them to admin addresses made of the admin prefix, the

15
00:01:02,646 --> 00:01:05,190
module, the controller, and the action.

16
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:11,109
The two views are files in the DemoNav folder, inside the Views folder of the module.

17
00:01:11,109 --> 00:01:15,085
Each holds a single paragraph, View One or View Two, so the page

18
00:01:15,085 --> 00:01:16,960
shows which item opened it.

19
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:25,530
The menu entries come from a class that implements INavigationProvider.

20
00:01:25,530 --> 00:01:29,957
By convention it is called AdminMenu and sits at the root of the module.

21
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:33,930
It takes an IStringLocalizer, so the captions can be translated.

22
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:39,672
BuildNavigationAsync receives the name of the menu being built, and a builder.

23
00:01:39,672 --> 00:01:43,772
The admin menu is named admin, so for any other menu the method

24
00:01:43,772 --> 00:01:46,030
returns without adding anything.

25
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:51,390
The builder holds the menu, and every provider adds its items to it.

26
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:56,250
Add takes a caption, a position, and a function that describes the item.

27
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:59,240
Adding inside another Add creates a child.

28
00:01:59,990 --> 00:02:02,315
Action gives an item its page:

29
00:02:02,315 --> 00:02:07,933
the action name, the controller name, and route values, where area is the module.

30
00:02:07,933 --> 00:02:12,970
My Root View has no page of its own, so selecting it opens its children.

31
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:21,086
The second argument of Add is the position, and items on the same

32
00:02:21,086 --> 00:02:22,685
level are sorted by it.

33
00:02:22,685 --> 00:02:24,229
Numbers sort by value.

34
00:02:24,229 --> 00:02:28,090
The anchors before and after sit at the two ends of the numbers.

35
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:33,926
PrefixPosition builds a text position from the caption, so sibling items sort by

36
00:02:33,926 --> 00:02:39,130
their translated captions, compared character by character without regard to case.

37
00:02:39,130 --> 00:02:43,980
A text position sorts after every number, even after the anchor named after.

38
00:02:44,730 --> 00:02:48,606
The positions start and end go before and after everything.

39
00:02:48,606 --> 00:02:53,317
The Tools and Settings items use end, so My Root View appears above them.

40
00:02:53,317 --> 00:02:57,790
A top-level item that must keep a fixed place takes a number instead.

41
00:03:01,990 --> 00:03:04,969
A provider takes part only when it is registered.

42
00:03:04,969 --> 00:03:09,574
In the Startup class of the module, AddNavigationProvider adds it as a service,

43
00:03:09,574 --> 00:03:12,770
and Orchard Core calls it each time it builds a menu.

44
00:03:14,470 --> 00:03:20,450
In the admin, open Tools, then Features, which lists the features of the installed modules.

45
00:03:21,526 --> 00:03:24,026
Search for MyModule, and select Enable.

46
00:03:24,026 --> 00:03:27,526
A message confirms that the feature MyModule was enabled.

47
00:03:27,997 --> 00:03:32,046
My Root View is in the admin menu, above Tools and Settings.

48
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:34,010
It has no page of its own:

49
00:03:34,010 --> 00:03:37,017
selecting it opens Child One and Child Two.

50
00:03:37,482 --> 00:03:38,737
Select Child One.

51
00:03:38,737 --> 00:03:42,668
The page opens in the admin theme with the paragraph View One, and

52
00:03:42,668 --> 00:03:44,852
the menu keeps Child One selected.

53
00:03:45,315 --> 00:03:51,025
Select Child Two, and the second view opens the same way, with the paragraph View Two.

54
00:03:55,225 --> 00:04:00,188
Most of the modules of Orchard Core extend AdminNavigationProvider instead.

55
00:04:00,188 --> 00:04:05,765
It checks the menu name for you, so you override BuildAsync and add your items there.

56
00:04:06,515 --> 00:04:09,112
Items with the same caption are merged.

57
00:04:09,112 --> 00:04:13,902
This provider, from the Background Tasks module, adds an item under Tools, so

58
00:04:13,902 --> 00:04:17,076
the Tools item that already exists gains a child.

59
00:04:17,076 --> 00:04:20,655
Your own provider can add to any existing item this way.

60
00:04:21,405 --> 00:04:26,226
Permission hides an item, and its children, from users who lack that permission.

61
00:04:26,226 --> 00:04:30,710
It does not protect the page, so the action checks the permission as well.

62
00:04:30,710 --> 00:04:33,065
A separate video covers permissions.

63
00:04:35,065 --> 00:04:37,960
That is how a module adds to the admin menu:

64
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:43,809
pages to open, a navigation provider that describes the items, and its registration in Startup.

65
00:04:43,809 --> 00:04:45,315
Thanks for watching.

